#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d2757f-1179-5ad6-b8c7-5165ac3a3eff" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "3459a04b-bb14-54ff-aaea-976fa542e3e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13269" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.67 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(-0.53 < Z < 0.53)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/0b30d484a778ffede28d541415b21e58.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$,\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z)\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= P(Z < 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.53) - P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $\\Phi(-0.53)$. However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΦ(-0.53) &= P(Z \\le - 0.53) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le 0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(0.53) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.7019 \\\\\n&= 0.2981\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(-0.53 < Z < 0.53) &= Φ(0.53) - Φ(-0.53) \\\\\n&= 0.7019 - 0.2981 \\\\\n&= 0.4038\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27018e18-9368-5171-ae78-097adc8bcddf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "3fe23c3b-29c8-5858-ae1d-b7a7aa34f76b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13270" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\gt -0.76)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8051 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8078 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/66fefa957dd168d1589cdb34bce3bced.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$ we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt -a) = P(Z \\lt a) = \\Phi(a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z > -0.76)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/1bc0f3dd71234ca7671872a937519a38.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\lt 0.76)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/bd8ed379c32619927570445941878c4f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > -0.76) = P(Z < 0.76) = \\Phi(0.76) = 0.7764\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a6ee4c7-a51f-5dce-b5c9-2e00acf6bf3e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "8b0db808-0cb9-5feb-b405-4ebab693bee5" :question/math-academy-id "e-13271" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9066 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9082 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9099 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9222 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9251 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9370 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9382 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/95c129d15152dcce16214fa0613e8fb6.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, we have the following symmetry property for $a > 0{:}$\n\n$$\nP(Z \\gt a) = P(Z \\lt -a) = \\Phi(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1)$, and we wish to compute $P(Z \\lt -1.54)$. The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/aa5d3354086f3622c4f7c5920b1753b8.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z \\gt 1.54)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4420/Source/Images/3e0d56a379a7fecf3403b9750a7133ab.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Z < - 1.54) &= P(Z > 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < 1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(1.54) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9382 \\\\\n&= 0.0618\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
